From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] up: when the driver pipeline cache is written during play (EDEN_UP_DRIVER_CACHE_EXIT_ONLY=1, EDEN_UP_DRIVER_CACHE_EVERY=<n>; waveydude's 0931), off by default

What: the driver's pipeline cache (vulkan_pipelines.bin) is written whole every 128 new pipelines,
at most every 30 s or one second per MiB of cache (QueueVulkanPipelineCacheFlush, on the
serialization thread): vkGetPipelineCacheData into one buffer, a .part file, a rename. For Ocarina
with about 8,700 pipelines that is about 123 MiB, so at most one write per ~123 s.

waveydude measured on a base PS5 (13.60), in a later area: the write took 24-55 s near the memory
limit, and all three of his stretches of ~960 ms frames (one every ~2 s) ran while a write was in
flight and ended within seconds of its log line (W-11: 261.3 s and 456.8 s; W-10: about 457 s).
Timing only: he has not run it with the switch on yet.

Switches (read once, at the first write or new pipeline):
- EDEN_UP_DRIVER_CACHE_EXIT_ONLY=1 (exact "1"): as his 0931. A new pipeline is still counted
  (EDEN_PE_PIPELINE_CACHE_EXIT's check at the end sees it as unsaved) and the function returns. The
  writes at boot (LoadDiskResources) and at the end of the session (~PipelineCache) are as before.
  Cost: a crash or power-off loses the driver builds made since boot; they are compiled again next
  session. Translations (vulkan.bin, the kept store) are other files and are not touched.
  Which ends write it (headless/main.cpp, stop_limit.h): every stop that reaches
  system.ShutdownMainProcess: Touchpad + L1, the test harness's test-quit, the game ending itself,
  a guest fault (also before its retry), a rendering failure. All of them run under the 10 s stop
  limit, counted from the stop's start; a stop still going at 10 s restarts the app
  (eden_restart_app), so an exit write that has not finished by then is lost (its .part is left,
  the old file stays). The exit write took ~3 s in a 9 Nov log, but the play writes took 24-55 s
  near the memory limit; with EXIT_ONLY a slow exit write loses every driver build since boot
  (the next start logs "did not stop ... within ten seconds"). Not written: a crash (the crash
  handler restarts the app), a freeze that ends in a kill, closing the app from the PS5 home
  screen (the system ends the process; the app has no handler for it), a power-off.
- EDEN_UP_DRIVER_CACHE_EVERY=<n> (digits only, 1-1000000; ours): every n new pipelines instead of 128. The time
  floor stays. 128 = the old pacing plus the timing line, for a control arm. Ignored with EXIT_ONLY.
- Either set: one line at start ("EDEN_UP_DRIVER_CACHE_EXIT_ONLY=1: ..." or
  "EDEN_UP_DRIVER_CACHE_EVERY=n: ..."), and after each successful write
  "EDEN_UP_DRIVER_CACHE: wrote N MiB in S.mmm s (K pipelines counted)" (LOG_INFO, Render_Vulkan),
  after the usual "Vulkan driver pipelines cached at:". The write began S s before that line.

Off (neither set): every 128 as before, no new lines; the only added work is one function-static
read per new pipeline and per write.

Not like 0931: our vk_pipeline_prep.cpp has no list of switches that change no translation, so
the names are left out of TranslationSwitches (setting them must not start an empty kept store);
our 0480's switch-list check is not in this tree. Not done: moving the write to fades or loads
(our load-wait code knows them): a write lasts 24-55 s, far longer than any fade or load,
so where it starts would not keep it out of play.

Origin and credit: waveydude, Eglantine-PS5- branch w13-driver-cache-exit-only, commit ae7294b,
patch 0931 (EXIT_ONLY and its log line). EDEN_UP_DRIVER_CACHE_EVERY and the timing line: ours.
